\section{Why 0.1, not 0.01}{

\code{bayesian_update()}'s \code{stats::density()} calls (kernel density estimation over
\code{seq(grid_min, grid_max, by = grid_resolution)}) are the dominant cost of
\code{fuse_general_kde()}'s per-cell loop - \code{Rprof()} profiling on a synthetic 10,000-cell raster
(PERFORMANCE_IMPROVEMENT_PLAN.md Tier 4) attributed 65\% of total wall-clock time to \code{density()}
(\code{dnorm}/\code{fft} internals) at the \code{0.01} default, vs. 12\% for the per-cell percentile-sampling
step. At \code{0.01}, a typical soil-property range (e.g. 20-50\%) produces a ~3,000-point evaluation
grid per cell, per side. Coarsening to \code{0.1} cuts that grid ~10x and measured \strong{~2.4x} faster
wall-clock on a real \code{fuse_adaptive()} call (23.86s -> 9.91s at 2,024 cells), while an accuracy
sweep across four representative percentile scenarios (narrow/wide/skewed distributions) found
the posterior mean/variance error at \code{0.1} stays under 0.02\%/0.12\% respectively vs. the \code{0.01}
reference - far inside the ~5-10\% variability typical of field-measured soil properties (the
same bar the analogous cubic-spline-to-linear-interpolation tradeoff used elsewhere in this
project's Python sibling report was validated against). Going coarser (\code{0.25}+) is where error
starts compounding fast (variance error reaches double digits to 470\% by \code{2.0}) - \code{0.1} is the
sweet spot, not an arbitrary round number.

\code{bayesian_update()}'s own standalone default is deliberately left at \code{0.01} - this constant
only overrides the grid resolution \code{fuse_general_kde()} requests, so any other direct caller of
\code{bayesian_update()} is unaffected.
